      "content": "Building an entire application through agentic vibe coding works remarkably well until high concurrency hits. When every pull request is generated by an autonomous coding agent that optimizes only for isolated unit tests, the overall system architecture silently rots.\n\nDuring a live event with hundreds of concurrent users, individual endpoints began timing out even though all health checks remained completely green. Every single function and database query looked fine in isolation, but the synthetic composition of agent-generated code created massive hidden serialization bottlenecks and redundant round trips.\n\nAI coding agents are exceptionally good at finding the shortest path to a diff that passes the immediate test suite. However, they lack a global mental model of system resource contention, caching dynamics, and distributed concurrency.\n\nTreating agents as black-box implementers works for prototypes, but scalable systems still demand hands-on architectural ownership and holistic performance profiling."
